P5.4 — reconnect / partial-loss drill, and a chunk-loss bug it found
specs/readiness-recovery-10-10/tasks.md P5.4

date (UTC): 2026-08-02
commit:     e7d18e8
setup:      real realtime-gateway with REALTIME_CHAOS_DROP_AFTER_CHUNKS=3, MAX_DROPS=2,
            real ml-inference behind it, scripts/chaos-realtime-reconnect.mjs as the client

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RECONNECT: PASS

  connect #1 (fresh single-use ticket) -> 3 chunks -> DROPPED by chaos
  backoff 456ms (equal jitter)
  connect #2 (fresh single-use ticket) -> 3 chunks -> DROPPED by chaos
  backoff 267ms
  connect #3 (fresh single-use ticket) -> 6 chunks -> clean close

  RESULT sent=12/12 acked=12 drops=2 tickets=3

  The reconnect contract holds: a fresh single-use ticket per connect, equal-jitter backoff,
  buffer-and-resume from the right chunk index. The client is told the session survived.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PARTIAL LOSS: FAIL — half the recitation was acknowledged and never stored

  chunks on disk:  6 of 12
  present:         ws-0000 ws-0001 ws-0002 ws-0003 ws-0004 ws-0005
  missing:         ws-0006 .. ws-0011

  Sampled at +0s, +1s, +2s, +5s and +10s with the gateway STILL RUNNING: 6 every time. This is not
  a teardown artifact and it is not a race that settles — the chunks never arrive.

  ── ROOT CAUSE: chunk ids COLLIDE across reconnects, and storage silently overwrites ──────────

  Nothing is lost in transit. A recording proxy between the gateway and ml-inference counted every
  POST /v1/audio-chunks and saw all TWELVE. What it also saw was the ids:

    drops=2, ids the gateway forwarded, in order:
      0000 0000 0000  0001 0001 0001  0002 0002 0002  0003 0004 0005

  Twelve chunks of DIFFERENT audio, six distinct ids. `services/realtime-gateway/src/lib.rs`:

    :1101   let mut sequence = 0_u64;                          <- per CONNECTION
    :1106   let chunk_id = format!("{session_id}-ws-{sequence:04}");

  `sequence` is declared inside the per-connection socket handler and restarts at zero on every
  reconnect, while `session_id` is deliberately the SAME across reconnects — that is what makes it
  one session. So connection 2's first chunk is handed the id connection 1's first chunk already
  used, ml-inference stores chunks under `<chunkId>.bin`, and the later write silently replaces the
  earlier audio.

  The arithmetic falls straight out and matches every run: distinct ids = 12 - 3*drops, and
  3 = REALTIME_CHAOS_DROP_AFTER_CHUNKS = how many ids each dropped connection burns through before
  the next one starts reusing them from zero.

    drops=0 -> 12 distinct -> 12/12 stored
    drops=1 ->  9 distinct ->  9/12 stored
    drops=2 ->  6 distinct ->  6/12 stored

  THE SAME COUNTER ALSO PRODUCES THE TIMESTAMP — which makes this worse than an id collision

    :1108   sequence * state.config.chunk_duration_ms          <- the chunk's startMs

    `sequence` restarting at zero restarts the SESSION CLOCK at zero. Connection 2's first chunk is
    not merely stored under connection 1's key; it claims to have been spoken at the same moment.

    That matters because ordering is by startMs, not by id (services/ml-inference assembles a
    session's audio sorted by startMs — see session-transcript.test.mjs, whose whole point is that
    filename order is arbitrary and spoken order is not). So making ids unique WITHOUT fixing the
    clock would stop the overwriting and leave a session whose chunks all claim overlapping
    timestamps: the recitation would be reassembled interleaved rather than in the order it was
    spoken, and the aligner would score a scrambled recitation.

    A per-connection nonce in the id is therefore NOT a sufficient fix. The gateway needs the
    session's high-water mark to survive the connection that produced it.

  WHAT IT MEANS FOR A LEARNER
    A learner whose connection blips mid-recitation OVERWRITES THEIR OWN EARLIER AUDIO. Not a
    dropped packet — the first part of the recitation is destroyed by the second, under the same
    key, with an ack for both and no error anywhere. The stored metadata (startMs/endMs) is
    replaced too, so what remains is not even a coherent prefix.

    Everything downstream then behaves correctly on corrupt input: transcription assembles what is
    there, and the aligner scores it against the FULL canonical passage. Words the learner did
    recite are recorded as words they missed.

  THE FIX IS NOT A ONE-LINER, which is why it is not in this commit
    `chunk_id` is not a local detail. It is the filename in audio storage, it is what
    `privacy_jobs.audio_object_keys_deleted` records for right-to-erasure, and it appears in the
    ack contract. And because the same counter produces startMs, the fix cannot be a per-connection
    nonce — the session's chunk high-water mark has to OUTLIVE the connection that produced it,
    which is a design choice with real options and real trade-offs:

      - an in-process map on Gateway keyed by session_id (needs eviction, like the ticket cache)
      - Redis, which the gateway already uses for session-state reconciliation
      - the client supplying its own resume index, which hands storage keys and the session clock
        to the client and should probably be refused on that basis alone

    Picking among those, keeping the erasure key contract intact, and proving it with the drill is
    a piece of work, not a patch. The erasure path is the one that must not be got wrong twice.

  ── three wrong hypotheses, recorded on purpose ──
  Before the proxy, this file successively blamed (1) the detached forwarder not draining on a clean
  close, (2) the socket handler returning early, (3) something shared consuming a fixed quantum per
  drop. All three were plausible, none was true, and the first would have sent someone to patch the
  close path. What settled it was not a better guess but an instrument: counting what actually
  crossed the wire.

  ── on the reliability of these measurements ──
  An earlier run read 0/12 and was a HARNESS ARTIFACT: the gateway had panicked at startup
  (ALLOW_INSECURE_DEFAULTS combined with METRICS_DEV_OPEN is refused) and the client had connected
  to a stale gateway from a previous run, still holding the port. Every run quoted here uses a
  unique port set, a fresh mktemp storage dir, and asserts a gateway OF THAT RUN is listening first.

NOT FIXED IN THIS RUN, deliberately. A drain fix on a detached task is the kind of concurrency
change that goes wrong when rushed at the end of a session. The reproduction is deterministic and
takes 20 seconds, so the fix can be written against a red test rather than against a hypothesis.

STILL NOT RUN for P5.4: long-audio, timeout, duplicate-delivery, recovery.
